help":{"markdown":"User-controlled data flows into EF.Property(). If attackers control the property name, this enables dynamic field access and ORM leak-style inference.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html)\n - [https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/](https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/)\n","text":"User-controlled data flows into EF.Property(). If attackers control the property name, this enables dynamic field access and ORM leak-style inference."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.orm-leak.ef-ormleak-taint-property","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.orm-leak.ef-ormleak-taint-property","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-203: Observable Discrepancy","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.orm-leak.ef-ormleak-taint-property"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"HTTP endpoint $ROUTE$PATH will handle $METHOD requests"},"help":{"markdown":"HTTP endpoint $ROUTE$PATH will handle $METHOD requests","text":"HTTP endpoint $ROUTE$PATH will handle $METHOD requests"},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.security.audit.entrypoints.aspnet-controller-entrypoint","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.security.audit.entrypoints.aspnet-controller-entrypoint","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["LOW CONFIDENCE"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.csharp.dotnet.security.audit.entrypoints.aspnet-controller-entrypoint"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode."},"help":{"markdown":"gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://github.com/gorilla/sessions/blob/v1.4.0/store.go#L59](https://github.com/gorilla/sessions/blob/v1.4.0/store.go#L59)\n","text":"gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-1275: Sensitive Cookie with Improper SameSite Attribute","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A02:2025 - Security Misconfiguration","OWASP-A05:2021 - Security Misconfiguration","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) — the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code."},"help":{"markdown":"An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) — the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#CrossOriginProtection](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#CrossOriginProtection)\n - [https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Decoder.Decode](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Decoder.Decode)\n","text":"An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) — the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"MarshalJSON is defined on a pointer receiver (*$T). Because encoding/json resolves json.Marshaler via reflection under strict method-set rules, a pointer-receiver MarshalJSON is NOT in the method set of the value type $T. Encoding a value of $T (or a struct containing a $T field) by value silently skips the custom marshaller and uses default marshalling. If the marshaller performs redaction/masking/field-omission, that protection disappears silently.\nAUDIT NOTE: this only becomes a bug if the type is somewhere encoded BY VALUE. On large codebases most pointer-receiver MarshalJSON are always encoded by pointer and therefore safe — this rule fires broadly and is intended as an audit aid, not a finding. To resolve a hit, check whether the type is ever passed by value to json.Marshal/Encode. Fix by moving MarshalJSON to a value receiver, or always storing/encoding a pointer."},"help":{"markdown":"MarshalJSON is defined on a pointer receiver (*$T). Because encoding/json resolves json.Marshaler via reflection under strict method-set rules, a pointer-receiver MarshalJSON is NOT in the method set of the value type $T. Encoding a value of $T (or a struct containing a $T field) by value silently skips the custom marshaller and uses default marshalling. If the marshaller performs redaction/masking/field-omission, that protection disappears silently.\nAUDIT NOTE: this only becomes a bug if the type is somewhere encoded BY VALUE. On large codebases most pointer-receiver MarshalJSON are always encoded by pointer and therefore safe — this rule fires broadly and is intended as an audit aid, not a finding. To resolve a hit, check whether the type is ever passed by value to json.Marshal/Encode. Fix by moving MarshalJSON to a value receiver, or always storing/encoding a pointer.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://github.com/golang/go/issues/22967](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/22967)\n - [https://go.dev/wiki/MethodSets](https://go.dev/wiki/MethodSets)\n","text":"MarshalJSON is defined on a pointer receiver (*$T). Because encoding/json resolves json.Marshaler via reflection under strict method-set rules, a pointer-receiver MarshalJSON is NOT in the method set of the value type $T. Encoding a value of $T (or a struct containing a $T field) by value silently skips the custom marshaller and uses default marshalling. If the marshaller performs redaction/masking/field-omission, that protection disappears silently.\nAUDIT NOTE: this only becomes a bug if the type is somewhere encoded BY VALUE. On large codebases most pointer-receiver MarshalJSON are always encoded by pointer and therefore safe — this rule fires broadly and is intended as an audit aid, not a finding. To resolve a hit, check whether the type is ever passed by value to json.Marshal/Encode. Fix by moving MarshalJSON to a value receiver, or always storing/encoding a pointer."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-marshaler-pointer-receiver","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-marshaler-pointer-receiver","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-marshaler-pointer-receiver"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads."},"help":{"markdown":"A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://media.defcon.org/DEF%20CON%2032/DEF%20CON%2032%20presentations/](https://media.defcon.org/DEF%20CON%2032/DEF%20CON%2032%20presentations/)\n - [https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go](https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go)\n","text":"A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound","MEDIUM CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A03:2021 - Injection","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"The result of len() is cast to a narrower fixed-width integer (uint32/int32/uint16/int16/uint8/int8). len() returns int (64-bit on amd64/arm64), so a length above the target type's max silently wraps. This is benign for in-memory counts/sizes but dangerous when the value becomes a length/size field in a binary or wire format, where a truncated length desynchronises the parser from the payload (data smuggling / boundary confusion). Validate the length before casting, or keep the full-width value and reject oversized input.\nNOTE: this broad rule fires on every narrowing len() cast and is INFO/audit. For the dangerous subset, see len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix below."},"help":{"markdown":"The result of len() is cast to a narrower fixed-width integer (uint32/int32/uint16/int16/uint8/int8). len() returns int (64-bit on amd64/arm64), so a length above the target type's max silently wraps. This is benign for in-memory counts/sizes but dangerous when the value becomes a length/size field in a binary or wire format, where a truncated length desynchronises the parser from the payload (data smuggling / boundary confusion). Validate the length before casting, or keep the full-width value and reject oversized input.\nNOTE: this broad rule fires on every narrowing len() cast and is INFO/audit. For the dangerous subset, see len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix below.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go](https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go)\n - [https://blog.trailofbits.com/2025/12/31/detect-gos-silent-arithmetic-bugs-with-go-panikint/](https://blog.trailofbits.com/2025/12/31/detect-gos-silent-arithmetic-bugs-with-go-panikint/)\n","text":"The result of len() is cast to a narrower fixed-width integer (uint32/int32/uint16/int16/uint8/int8). len() returns int (64-bit on amd64/arm64), so a length above the target type's max silently wraps. This is benign for in-memory counts/sizes but dangerous when the value becomes a length/size field in a binary or wire format, where a truncated length desynchronises the parser from the payload (data smuggling / boundary confusion). Validate the length before casting, or keep the full-width value and reject oversized input.\nNOTE: this broad rule fires on every narrowing len() cast and is INFO/audit. For the dangerous subset, see len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix below."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-to-narrow-int-overflow","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-to-narrow-int-overflow","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A03:2021 - Injection","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-to-narrow-int-overflow"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"A string is compared against a PRIVILEGED account name (admin/root/etc.) and then passed to a C-interop / LDAP / exec sink in the same function, with no null-byte rejection. Go strings may contain \\x00; C-style consumers truncate at the first null byte. An input like \"admin\\x00x\" fails the Go equality check yet resolves to \"admin\" past the boundary, bypassing the gate. Reject null bytes (strings.ContainsRune(s, 0) / strings.IndexByte(s, 0) != -1) before any comparison or interop call at the trust boundary.\nv2: the gate must compare against a non-empty privileged-looking literal (not `== \"\"` or arbitrary config values), and the sink must be a real C call, os/exec, or a named LDAP/PAM package — narrowed after real-code runs where empty-string defaults and cloud-SDK method names produced false positives."},"help":{"markdown":"A string is compared against a PRIVILEGED account name (admin/root/etc.) and then passed to a C-interop / LDAP / exec sink in the same function, with no null-byte rejection. Go strings may contain \\x00; C-style consumers truncate at the first null byte. An input like \"admin\\x00x\" fails the Go equality check yet resolves to \"admin\" past the boundary, bypassing the gate. Reject null bytes (strings.ContainsRune(s, 0) / strings.IndexByte(s, 0) != -1) before any comparison or interop call at the trust boundary.\nv2: the gate must compare against a non-empty privileged-looking literal (not `== \"\"` or arbitrary config values), and the sink must be a real C call, os/exec, or a named LDAP/PAM package — narrowed after real-code runs where empty-string defaults and cloud-SDK method names produced false positives.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/golang-codereview](https://www.elttam.com/blog/golang-codereview)\n","text":"A string is compared against a PRIVILEGED account name (admin/root/etc.) and then passed to a C-interop / LDAP / exec sink in the same function, with no null-byte rejection. Go strings may contain \\x00; C-style consumers truncate at the first null byte. An input like \"admin\\x00x\" fails the Go equality check yet resolves to \"admin\" past the boundary, bypassing the gate. Reject null bytes (strings.ContainsRune(s, 0) / strings.IndexByte(s, 0) != -1) before any comparison or interop call at the trust boundary.\nv2: the gate must compare against a non-empty privileged-looking literal (not `== \"\"` or arbitrary config values), and the sink must be a real C call, os/exec, or a named LDAP/PAM package — narrowed after real-code runs where empty-string defaults and cloud-SDK method names produced false positives."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.missing-null-byte-check-at-trust-boundary","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.missing-null-byte-check-at-trust-boundary","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-158: Improper Neutralization of Null Byte or NUL Character","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A07:2021 - Identification and Authentication Failures","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.missing-null-byte-check-at-trust-boundary"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"A *url.URL pointer held in a variable or field is aliased (not copied) and then a field of it is mutated. Assigning a *url.URL copies the pointer, not the struct, so the write mutates the shared original. If that original is package-level, a struct field, or otherwise shared across requests or goroutines, this corrupts the URL seen elsewhere (open-redirect / parameter-smuggling / data race).\nThis rule only fires when the alias source is a bare identifier (e.g. `u := s.someURL`) — the shape most likely to be a shared pointer. It does NOT fire on fresh allocations, which are safe: value composite literals (`url.URL{}`), dereference copies (`*p`), or any function/method call result (`url.Parse(...)`, `r.URL()`, `x.Copy()`, `ResolveReference(...)`), because those return a new value/struct that is not shared.\nFix: copy the struct (`u := *original`) or build a fresh &url.URL{...}."},"help":{"markdown":"A *url.URL pointer held in a variable or field is aliased (not copied) and then a field of it is mutated. Assigning a *url.URL copies the pointer, not the struct, so the write mutates the shared original. If that original is package-level, a struct field, or otherwise shared across requests or goroutines, this corrupts the URL seen elsewhere (open-redirect / parameter-smuggling / data race).\nThis rule only fires when the alias source is a bare identifier (e.g. `u := s.someURL`) — the shape most likely to be a shared pointer. It does NOT fire on fresh allocations, which are safe: value composite literals (`url.URL{}`), dereference copies (`*p`), or any function/method call result (`url.Parse(...)`, `r.URL()`, `x.Copy()`, `ResolveReference(...)`), because those return a new value/struct that is not shared.\nFix: copy the struct (`u := *original`) or build a fresh &url.URL{...}.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://pkg.go.dev/net/url#URL](https://pkg.go.dev/net/url#URL)\n","text":"A *url.URL pointer held in a variable or field is aliased (not copied) and then a field of it is mutated. Assigning a *url.URL copies the pointer, not the struct, so the write mutates the shared original. If that original is package-level, a struct field, or otherwise shared across requests or goroutines, this corrupts the URL seen elsewhere (open-redirect / parameter-smuggling / data race).\nThis rule only fires when the alias source is a bare identifier (e.g. `u := s.someURL`) — the shape most likely to be a shared pointer. It does NOT fire on fresh allocations, which are safe: value composite literals (`url.URL{}`), dereference copies (`*p`), or any function/method call result (`url.Parse(...)`, `r.URL()`, `x.Copy()`, `ResolveReference(...)`), because those return a new value/struct that is not shared.\nFix: copy the struct (`u := *original`) or build a fresh &url.URL{...}."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.net-url-pointer-alias-mutation","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.net-url-pointer-alias-mutation","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-362: Concurrent Execution using Shared Resource with Improper Synchronization ('Race Condition')","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.net-url-pointer-alias-mutation"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Dynamic Beego ORM expression string passed to Filter/Exclude/Condition.*. Review whether the expression can include attacker-controlled \"__\" traversal or operator suffixes (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\")."},"help":{"markdown":"Dynamic Beego ORM expression string passed to Filter/Exclude/Condition.*. Review whether the expression can include attacker-controlled \"__\" traversal or operator suffixes (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\").\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html)\n - [https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/](https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/)\n","text":"Dynamic Beego ORM expression string passed to Filter/Exclude/Condition.*. Review whether the expression can include attacker-controlled \"__\" traversal or operator suffixes (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\")."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-expr-review","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-expr-review","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-203: Observable Discrepancy","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-expr-review"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Dynamic OrderBy expression. Beego supports ordering on related fields and \"-\" prefix for descending order. Review attacker control and inference/oracle risk."},"help":{"markdown":"Dynamic OrderBy expression. Beego supports ordering on related fields and \"-\" prefix for descending order. Review attacker control and inference/oracle risk.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html)\n - [https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/](https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/)\n","text":"Dynamic OrderBy expression. Beego supports ordering on related fields and \"-\" prefix for descending order. Review attacker control and inference/oracle risk."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-orderby-review","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-orderby-review","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-203: Observable Discrepancy","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-dynamic-orderby-review"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Beego ORM FilterRaw used. Docs warn it treats input directly as a query condition and Beego performs no checks. Review carefully (can enable injection or leak oracles)."},"help":{"markdown":"Beego ORM FilterRaw used. Docs warn it treats input directly as a query condition and Beego performs no checks. Review carefully (can enable injection or leak oracles).\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html)\n - [https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/](https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/)\n","text":"Beego ORM FilterRaw used. Docs warn it treats input directly as a query condition and Beego performs no checks. Review carefully (can enable injection or leak oracles)."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-filterraw","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-filterraw","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-203: Observable Discrepancy","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-filterraw"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"User-controlled input flows into Beego ORM query expression (Filter/Exclude/Condition.*). Beego expressions support \"__\" traversal and operators (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\"), so attacker-controlled expressions can enable ORM leak-style inference."},"help":{"markdown":"User-controlled input flows into Beego ORM query expression (Filter/Exclude/Condition.*). Beego expressions support \"__\" traversal and operators (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\"), so attacker-controlled expressions can enable ORM leak-style inference.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/203.html)\n - [https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n - [https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/](https://www.elttam.com/blog/leaking-more-than-you-joined-for/)\n","text":"User-controlled input flows into Beego ORM query expression (Filter/Exclude/Condition.*). Beego expressions support \"__\" traversal and operators (e.g. \"__contains\", \"__gt\"), so attacker-controlled expressions can enable ORM leak-style inference."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-taint-dynamic-expr","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-taint-dynamic-expr","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-203: Observable Discrepancy","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.orm-leak.beego-ormleak-taint-dynamic-expr"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Jackson Polymorphic Typing: This rule is to detect any instances of polymorphic typing to aid in determining vulnerable Jackson deserialization issues. Check if deserializing untrusted JSON to a nominal type such as `java.lang.Object` or permissive tag interfaces such as `java.io.Serializable` or `java.lang.Comparable` is occuring."},"help":{"markdown":"Jackson Polymorphic Typing: This rule is to detect any instances of polymorphic typing to aid in determining vulnerable Jackson deserialization issues. Check if deserializing untrusted JSON to a nominal type such as `java.lang.Object` or permissive tag interfaces such as `java.io.Serializable` or `java.lang.Comparable` is occuring.","text":"Jackson Polymorphic Typing: This rule is to detect any instances of polymorphic typing to aid in determining vulnerable Jackson deserialization issues. Check if deserializing untrusted JSON to a nominal type such as `java.lang.Object` or permissive tag interfaces such as `java.io.Serializable` or `java.lang.Comparable` is occuring."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.polymorphic-typing","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.polymorphic-typing","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A8: Insecure Deserialization","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.polymorphic-typing"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"$X Jackson Polymorphic Typing: Polymorphic typing may have been introduced through overriding the default Jackson type resolver."},"help":{"markdown":"$X Jackson Polymorphic Typing: Polymorphic typing may have been introduced through overriding the default Jackson type resolver.","text":"$X Jackson Polymorphic Typing: Polymorphic typing may have been introduced through overriding the default Jackson type resolver."},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.type-resolver-override","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.type-resolver-override","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data","LOW CONFIDENCE","OWASP-A8: Insecure Deserialization","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jackson.type-resolver-override"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS"},"help":{"markdown":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS","text":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS"},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jax-rs.security.audit.entrypoints.consumes-class-star","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jax-rs.security.audit.entrypoints.consumes-class-star","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["LOW CONFIDENCE"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Semgrep Finding: security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jax-rs.security.audit.entrypoints.consumes-class-star"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS.$METHOD"},"help":{"markdown":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS.$METHOD","text":"JAX-RS @Consumes(\"*/*\") $CLASS.$METHOD"},"id":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jax-rs.security.audit.entrypoints.consumes-method-star","name":"security-audit.semgrep.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.java.jax-rs.security.audit.entrypoints.consumes-method-star","properties":{"precision":"very-hig